OURS (not proposed to Guillaume Duboc yet). The solve phase of tallying builds the
solution as one system of mutually recursive nodes (_claude/INFERENCE-SCALING.md of the
paper, §6.11-6.12). A saturated set gives one equation per variable,
a = (lower or a') and upper with a' fresh. solve/1 solved them one after the other,
substituting each recursive solution into the others and back: the solution was a nest
of recursive systems, on which every later emptiness check, simplification and printing
blew up. Now one recursive/1 system has a node per variable whose equation mentions a
variable of the set, and each body substitutes, in one structural substitution, every
variable of the set by its node (or by its solution, when its equation mentions none)
and every a' by a. In a normalized set the bounds of a only have larger variables at the
top level, so the nodes are well defined; the sequential solve is kept as a fallback
when top-level occurrences form a cycle (never seen). The fallback, too, renames without
simplifying, and solve_recursive_equation/2 uses syntactic_vars/1 instead of vars/1.
It assumes that no recursive node whose unfolding has top-level variables is a top-level
leaf of a constraint (normalize_summand/2 does not unfold such leaves; the code before
this patch fails on them too); the types of the prototype satisfy it (checked by a
proof-checker run, _claude/prep/solve-system/CHECK.md).
